## Scheduling mechanism: `startOn` requested (ContextHub, critical)

The `[run-not-before …]` title marker used by this chain is **interim**. The permanent mechanism has been raised as a **critical** ContextHub item — **`WL-0MWM0FDZK007BAD0`** ([critical] Add startOn (earliest start date/time) to work items; all CLI and Herdr searches filter out not-yet-started items by default):

- an ISO-8601 UTC `startOn` field per item, **defaulting to `createdAt`**;
- `wl create --start-on` / `wl update --start-on`;
- `wl list`, `wl search`, `wl next`, `wl in_progress`, `wl recent` and the Herdr worklist/dispatcher **filter out future items by default** (opt-in `--include-not-started`), with `wl show <id>` never filtered;
- a claim guard (`start_on_not_reached`) with an audited override, migration, sync round-trip, tests and docs.

**Why it matters to this epic specifically:** five of these nine children are date-gated windows (V1 now; V2 and V3 when their predecessor's restart is known). Today the only way to express that is a title prefix that nothing filters on and that has to be rewritten at every restart — and a restart that lands inside a window silently destroys a week of measurement. When `WL-0MWM0FDZK007BAD0` ships, replace each title marker with `--start-on` and record the change here.

No dependency edge is wired from this chain to the ContextHub item: the title markers keep the chain correct in the meantime, and blocking measurement work on a CLI feature would strand it.
